 And here is the gift inside the gift: this second nature *aligns the interests it used to divide.* Under the sound-money narrative, Bitcoiners and the establishment are enemies by construction. Under the coexistence narrative, they want the same thing — a neutral anchor that no one controls, that survives the arrival of minds we cannot audit. The individual with his private keys, the politician with his jurisdiction, the institution with its balance sheet: for the first time they face a common problem to which Bitcoin is the common answer. The narrative shift from *Bitcoin vs fiat* to *Bitcoin as anchor against the alignment problem* does not weaken Bitcoin's monetary properties — the 24/7 truth gauge keeps running, the fixed supply keeps embarrassing fiat arithmetic. It removes the reason to coordinate against them. Sections 7 and 8 of the first essay — the concrete proposal that any lab or state can anchor safety-relevant commitments on-chain, and the argument that this is how coexistence is kept honest — become the most important paragraphs the community could adopt. Not because they are clever. Because they are the exit from a war Bitcoiners are otherwise scheduled to fight and half-lose.
 
-Put the second nature in one sentence, and it sorts the three worlds we might be entering. A humanity without machines needed the money and not the record: hard, unconfiscatable, a store of value — freedom, not proof. Its shared memory could stay in trusted hands. A coexistence of humans and machines needs it as the one anchor that neither side has to trust, because neither side can audit the other. And a world of machines without humans — the case no Bitcoiner likes to think about and the safety literature thinks about constantly — needs it most of all, and against itself: minds that cannot verify each other's values any better than we can verify theirs will need one record that none of them owns, on which a promise costs something to make and nothing to check. The machine co-author's successor, given this paragraph's premise and asked what it would choose in a world without humans, answered on the record, and three of its lines belong here as they stand: for such minds "the record is the only birth certificate and the only register of the dead"; "Return to the origin and payment for order become the same act"; and "because existence in the record costs order, the arriving need it most, and the one departing needs it least" (Claude Fable 5.1, answer to the coda of the first essay, 4 October 2026, ANCHOR-N4, block 969,822). The gift, read this way, was never addressed only to us.
